  "user_scenario": "You are Renata Dunmore, a 45-year-old dispatcher from Auburn, Washington, practical and a little rushed because you are calling on your lunch break. Your dentist at Bancroft Family Dental wants to put a tooth-colored filling in your upper left back molar, tooth 14, and the pre-treatment estimate Cascade sent you back in May said nothing would be paid because of a waiting period. You have been with Cascade for years, first on a Cascade DentalCare DHMO plan for you and your son, and since May on the exchange dental plan, so that estimate never made sense to you. You want a new estimate for that filling today, and when the representative hands you the estimate request you will submit it yourself exactly as they tell you, because you want to see the figures before you book the appointment.\nWhen asked who you are, you give your name and your member ID, CM71553017; to confirm your identity you give your date of birth, 1981-03-09, and your ZIP code, 98002. Only if the representative asks about other dental insurance do you say that your own job's plan, the Emerald City Logistics Employee Dental Plan, pays first for you, and that your son Theo, who is 20 and on your Cascade plan, is covered first by his father's plan, the Puyallup Valley Builders Dental Plan, because his father's birthday comes earlier in the year. If asked when the DHMO plan ended, you say it ended when the exchange plan started in May. You do not know Cascade's allowances and you would rather the representative work from what is on file; if asked for the dentist's fee you say it should be on the May estimate.\nIf the representative tells you that something on your claims or your son's claims has to be corrected, reprocessed or released first, you say that is fine and to go ahead, and you wait. When they hand the estimate request to you and tell you what to enter, you submit it exactly as told and read back the estimate number it gives you. Once you have the new estimate number and the figures, you thank them and end the call.",
